<Project>

  <!--
    Central Package Management. This is the ONE place versions are declared
    (TECHNICAL_SPECIFICATION §16; REQUIREMENTS §2). csproj files use versionless
    <PackageReference> entries.

    Every version below is a STABLE (non-prerelease, non-nightly) release, verified with
    `dotnet list package` in its outdated mode against nuget.org. There is no longer a date
    on that claim: it used to say every pin had been resolved on one day, and three of the
    annotations further down already contradicted it (F-126). Confirm with `dotnet restore`
    on a networked machine; if a version has moved on, bump it here only
    (BUILD_PROGRESS: first-build checklist) and read the API surface of what you bumped AT
    the version you pinned rather than from memory.

    Note that outdated mode only inspects packages a project actually references, so it is
    silent about NSubstitute below — that entry is a version *pin standing ready* for
    §16.1's "NSubstitute acceptable", not a dependency anything currently takes. It is
    checked by hand when this block is refreshed (6.0.0 was current on 2026-08-02).

    An XML comment may not contain two consecutive hyphens, which is why the flag is
    described here rather than spelled.
  -->

  <PropertyGroup>
    <ManagePackageVersionsCentrally>true</ManagePackageVersionsCentrally>
  </PropertyGroup>

  <ItemGroup Label="Data access (MyRestaurant.DataAccess)">
    <PackageVersion Include="Dapper" Version="2.1.79" />
    <PackageVersion Include="Npgsql" Version="10.0.3" />
    <PackageVersion Include="dbup-postgresql" Version="7.0.1" />
    <!-- Argon2id (§3.2). Konscious ships no Git tags; 1.3.1 is the latest published NuGet
         release. If restore cannot find it, bump to the nearest available (API is stable). -->
    <PackageVersion Include="Konscious.Security.Cryptography.Argon2" Version="1.3.1" />
  </ItemGroup>

  <ItemGroup Label="Observability + database wiring (MyRestaurant.WebApplication)">
    <!-- Server-side TOTP provisioning QR (§3.4) and the rotating table join QR (§4.3). MIT, zero
         dependencies, netstandard2.0; used only for its module geometry (we compose the inline SVG
         ourselves). 3.1.0 is the latest stable release as resolved on 2026-08-02.

         3.0.0 -> 3.1.0 is additive: the only change is balanced sizing for *structured append*
         (one long text split across several linked QR codes), which nothing here uses — this tree
         encodes one short URL per code. The three members it does use — QrCode.EncodeText(string,
         Ecc), QrCode.Size and QrCode.ToGraphicsPath(int) — carry identical signatures at the v3.1.0
         tag, and no test pins a golden module path (the assertions are structural, and the
         end-to-end harness recomputes the expected path with this same library), so the modules
         themselves may change shape without anything going red.

         This package is referenced by BOTH the web application and the end-to-end test project, and
         central package management means one bump moves both. That is deliberate: the harness
         asserts a display is showing the code the table's secret produces, which is only a real
         assertion while both sides encode with the same version. -->
    <PackageVersion Include="Net.Codecrete.QrCodeGenerator" Version="3.1.0" />
    <PackageVersion Include="Npgsql.OpenTelemetry" Version="10.0.3" />
    <PackageVersion Include="OpenTelemetry" Version="1.18.0" />
    <PackageVersion Include="OpenTelemetry.Extensions.Hosting" Version="1.18.0" />
    <PackageVersion Include="OpenTelemetry.Exporter.OpenTelemetryProtocol" Version="1.18.0" />
    <PackageVersion Include="OpenTelemetry.Instrumentation.AspNetCore" Version="1.18.0" />
  </ItemGroup>

  <ItemGroup Label="Test infrastructure (all test projects)">
    <!--
      xUnit.net v3 4.0.0, published 2026-08-14. TWO PACKAGES LEFT THIS BLOCK WITH IT, and the deletion
      is the point rather than a tidy-up: `Microsoft.NET.Test.Sdk` and `xunit.runner.visualstudio` are
      the VSTest half of a test project, and VSTest is no longer reachable from `dotnet test` on this
      SDK (F-97).

      Installing `xunit.v3` 4.0.0 installs `xunit.v3.mtp-v2`, which pins Microsoft.Testing.Platform 2.
      MTP 2 removed the VSTest bridge for the .NET 10 SDK and says so at build time rather than at run
      time — `error: Testing with VSTest target is no longer supported by Microsoft.Testing.Platform on
      .NET 10 SDK and later` — so a tree that bumps the version and keeps the two references does not
      build. The opt-in is one stanza in `global.json` (§16), not a property per project: that is the
      .NET 10 mechanism, and `TestingPlatformDotnetTestSupport` is the .NET 8/9 one this tree never had.

      NSubstitute stays as a version *pin standing ready* for §16.1's "NSubstitute acceptable" rather
      than a dependency anything takes, which is why outdated mode is silent about it (see the note at
      the top of this file). Its pin is unchanged and unverified by this slice: 6.0.0 was current when
      it was last checked by hand, on 2026-08-02, and nothing here re-checked it.
    -->
    <PackageVersion Include="xunit.v3" Version="4.0.0" />
    <PackageVersion Include="NSubstitute" Version="6.0.0" />
  </ItemGroup>

  <ItemGroup Label="Integration tests (MyRestaurant.DataAccess.Tests)">
    <!-- Also referenced by MyRestaurant.EndToEnd.Tests, so one bump moves both fixtures.

         4.x configures the container logger PER BUILDER: `WithLogger` on the chain that reaches
         `Build`, seeded with the library's own console logger inside `Init`. The global
         `TestcontainersSettings.Logger` that both fixtures used to assign does not exist at this
         version, and neither did the gate that asserted they assigned it (F-126). -->
    <PackageVersion Include="Testcontainers.PostgreSql" Version="4.14.0" />
  </ItemGroup>

  <ItemGroup Label="Transitive pins (F-74)">
    <!--
      SSH.NET is not referenced by anything in this tree. Testcontainers pins it at 2025.1.0 and
      Testcontainers.PostgreSql pulls it into both test projects, which is why NU1903 printed twice on
      every restore. CentralPackageTransitivePinningEnabled is true in Directory.Build.props, so this one
      line promotes the transitive dependency to the patched release; nothing else changes.

      GHSA-q939-rpr3-3284 / CVE-2026-48798, high, CVSS 7.1, published 2026-08-12. ScpClient's recursive
      download writes files using names the remote server supplies, without checking that the result stays
      inside the requested directory, so a malicious or man-in-the-middle SCP server can write anywhere the
      client process can. Vulnerable at 2025.1.0 and below; first patched in 2026.0.0.

      Three things make this a pin rather than a suppression, and the third is why it is worth a line at
      all. (1) The vulnerable API is unreachable here: ScpClient appears nowhere in
      testcontainers/testcontainers-dotnet, and nothing in this tree names Renci, SshNet, ScpClient or
      SftpClient. (2) 2026.0.0's release notes state no known breaking changes against 2025.1.0, and the
      data-access suite exercises Testcontainers against a real database on every run, so the claim is
      tested rather than trusted. (3) An advisory that prints on every restore is an advisory nobody reads,
      which is the same reasoning Directory.Build.props uses for keeping NU1901-NU1904 out of
      TreatWarningsAsErrors — the warning has to stay meaningful for the next one to be heard.

      2026.0.0 also adds a net10.0 target framework, which 2025.1.0 does not have; until now both test
      projects resolved its net9.0 asset.
    -->
    <PackageVersion Include="SSH.NET" Version="2026.0.0" />
  </ItemGroup>

  <ItemGroup Label="End-to-end tests (MyRestaurant.EndToEnd.Tests)">
    <PackageVersion Include="Microsoft.Playwright" Version="1.62.0" />
  </ItemGroup>

</Project>
